!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/schema/invalid/a-constructor-marker-is-not-grammar-expected.tn"
!!schema:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/schema-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §12.1, [TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1"
  description: """
    There is no constructor marker. A `~` at the start of a type-def body is not a production of the
    schema grammar, so this fails in the parser rather than resolving to a constructor -- which is the
    observable difference between a revision that carries the marker and one that does not.

    What makes an entry a constructor is that it IS-A `top` (§4.1): it composes, transitively, with a base
    kind, which its supertype chain records. Dropping the `~` from this very declaration leaves a valid
    constructor declaration, refused for a different reason -- §2.2.2, since this subject is governed by
    meta.tn and not by the meta-kernel.

    Category is `resolver` even though the failure is a parse one: §8.1 makes every error that stops a
    schema loading a resolver error, however early the phase that caught it.
    """
  meta: "meta.tn"
  import: ["core.tn"]
  error: {
    category: resolver
  }
}
